Based solely on current estimated mortality rates for 2008 and population projections, it is estimated there will be over 13.2 million deaths from cancer [49].

In 2008, the World Health Organization (WHO) identified cancer as one of the four leading threats to human health and development (along with cardiovascular diseases, chronic respiratory diseases and diabetes). The WHO states that the global burden of cancer can be reduced and controlled by implementing three evidence-based strategies: preventing cancer from occurring in the first place, detecting cancer earlier and managing patients with cancer [50].